  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This recipe makes 4 bowls and takes around 15 minutes from start to finish."
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Miso soup is the backbone of Japanese home cooking. It is served at breakfast, lunch, and dinner."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Never boil the soup after adding the"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "miso"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ". Boiling kills the beneficial bacteria and dulls the flavour. Dissolve the miso off the heat, then serve straight away."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What you need"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "ingredientsList",
        "attrs": {
          "defaultServings": 4,
          "items": [
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "dashi-stock",
              "amount": 800,
              "unit": "ml",
              "prepNote": "see tip below for quick dashi",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "miso-white",
              "amount": 40,
              "unit": "g",
              "prepNote": null,
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "tofu-silken",
              "amount": 200,
              "unit": "g",
              "prepNote": "cut into 1cm cubes",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "wakame",
              "amount": 8,
              "unit": "g",
              "prepNote": "dried, soaked in cold water for 5 minutes and drained",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "spring-onion",
              "amount": 3,
              "unit": null,
              "prepNote": "finely sliced",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour the"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "dashi"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "into a medium saucepan. Warm over a medium heat until just steaming. Do not let it boil."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Spoon the"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "miso"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "into a small bowl. Ladle about 4 tablespoons of hot dashi over it and whisk until smooth."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the tofu cubes and drained"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "wakame"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "to the pan. Warm gently for 1 minute."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the pan from the heat. Stir in the dissolved miso mixture. Do not return to the hob."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Ladle into bowls. Top with sliced spring onions and serve immediately."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Soup tastes flat or salty without depth",
              "cause": "The dashi was weak or made with water alone.",
              "fix": "Use proper dashi made with kombu and bonito flakes, or a good-quality instant dashi powder. Tap water with miso alone lacks umami."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Tofu breaks apart in the soup",
              "cause": "Silken tofu is too fragile for stirring.",
              "fix": "Add the tofu after the dashi is hot and do not stir once it goes in. Use a gentle ladle motion to serve."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Soup tastes dull after standing",
              "cause": "Miso flavour fades when it sits too long or gets reheated.",
              "fix": "Make and serve miso soup fresh. If you must reheat, warm gently and never let it boil."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Miso soup is eaten across all of Japan, every day. It appears at the breakfast table alongside rice and pickles. Regional versions vary the miso type and the ingredients added. White miso is common in Kyoto. Red miso is traditional in Nagoya. Coastal areas often add clams or small fish. At home, it is whatever is in the fridge."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}